# Local biomedical image review workspace: research proposal

**Status:** Research-stage plan, not a built or qualified application. Source facts, engineering inferences, product choices, and proposed validation are distinguished below. Source keys resolve in `sources.json`; executed check W1 and unexecuted checks are recorded in `witnesses.json`.

## Recommendation and precedents

Build a single-writer desktop project with read-only originals, bounded region access, and an application-owned versioned project/annotation schema. For the initial support boundary, evaluate tifffile 2026.9.20 for eligible local OME-TIFF/BigTIFF region reads, ome-zarr-py 0.20.1 for eligible local OME-NGFF 0.4/Zarr v2 data, and napari 0.8.0 layer/viewer components in a Qt 6 shell [S1, S3, S4, S5]. Do not treat a viewer session file as the scientific project contract.

**Independent implementation precedents.** tifffile documents a route from TIFF pages, strips, tiles and pyramidal levels to Zarr-style region slicing (`imread(..., return_as='zarr')`); it is a TIFF storage reader and documents format/codec limitations [S3]. ome-zarr-py separately interprets NGFF hierarchy, axes, per-level transforms, channel metadata and labels, and loads resolution arrays as Dask-backed data [S4]. These solve different problems: a TIFF need not be NGFF, and an NGFF group needs semantic interpretation beyond TIFF indexing. napari supplies a third useful precedent: interactive layers and coordinates/transforms for points, shapes and labels [S5]. These source examples do not prove an integrated app works.

**Issue/fix/regression chain.** napari issue #9047 reports xarray-backed label painting failing in 0.7.1rc4: painting reached fancy-indexing code and an xarray path raised a `numpy.bool`/shape `TypeError`. Issue comments connect the report to PR #8636 and record confirmation. The merged PR changed paint updates to boolean masks and bounded read-modify-write regions, and added tests across array backends. The specific `test_paint_2d_xarray` in the v0.8.0 test file paints an xarray-backed label image and checks it remains xarray-backed and changes the expected region. PR #8636 merged as `63b1239ab53dedc4e40b934ee79fd18e4147dd83`, was included in v0.8.0, and the regression test is present in that tag [S6]. This chain establishes a real repair and released regression test; it supports bounded mask edits and tests per backend. Its limits: the reported xarray case was specific; the test is in-memory and uses a timeout only as an indirect memory signal. It establishes neither 3 GB disk-backed TIFF behavior nor app-level persistence, all backends, or target responsiveness.

**Pin/integration caveat.** ome-zarr-py v0.20.1 metadata requires Python >=3.12 and calls the project beta; use Python 3.12 as the initial floor. napari v0.8.0 and tifffile 2026.9.20 are proposed source pins. Resolve exact transitive versions and one Qt 6 binding before implementation; no compatible environment or GUI integration was built here [S3–S5].

## Minimum workflow

1. Create a project directory with a stable project UUID and schema version. At import, offer an explicit choice to reference the external original or make a verified project copy. Never rewrite originals. A copy costs disk and time; references are smaller but depend on external paths remaining available.
2. Stage imports as `importing`; show progress and cancellation during header/metadata read, streaming identity hash and optional bundle copy. Record source locator, byte length, SHA-256, parser/version and import time. Retain raw OME-XML/TIFF tags or original NGFF attributes; store parsed data and user corrections separately. Show axes/order, dtype, dimensions, calibration, orientation, channels and time with known/unknown/overridden state. Ambiguity produces `needs_axes`; unsupported inputs produce `unsupported`; interrupted inputs remain `incomplete`. Never guess silently or delete the source.
3. Navigate by named channel, slice and time axes. Read only view regions/chunks/planes. Channel visibility/color, window/level, gamma and LUT belong to persisted view state; they never write image samples. Clearly label source and derived data. Show progress and permit cancellation on reads, copies, import and export.
4. Annotate points, polygons/shapes and integer masks. Provide reusable styles, keyboard navigation, undo/redo and slice/time, volume or acquisition scope. Persist changes as app transactions; mask writes should touch bounded regions/chunks and record reversible data. napari's issue chain shows why array indexing assumptions need tests [S6].
5. Save, close and reopen on another machine. Resolve by source identity, not path. Report missing or changed files and preserve annotation associations until explicit user action.
6. Export an annotation table, optional mask overlay and small derived image. Summarize exactly what each output preserves, transforms and omits; verify source identity remains unchanged.

## Data contract: identity, axes, coordinates and provenance

**Product choice — identity.** Each source and derived asset receives a stable UUID. For a file source, identity includes full streaming SHA-256 and byte length; a path is only a locator. A canceled hash remains unverified. On relocation, hash the selected file before rebinding. A mismatch keeps the original project record and offers import-as-new-source; never silently attach prior annotations to different bytes.

**Product choice — axes/calibration.** Keep native array axis order and names; initial accepted axes are named `t`, `c`, `z`, `y`, `x`, only where metadata and array shape agree. Display order is separate. Do not infer unknown `S`, `Q`, `m` or custom axes. Allow a recorded explicit mapping only when it makes the structure unambiguous; retain raw metadata plus correction, author, time and reason. Otherwise leave unresolved. OME-TIFF uses OME-XML plane mapping and can refer to multiple UUID-linked files, so the first version should limit multi-file import [S2]. NGFF 0.4 names axes and provides scale/translation coordinate transforms with defined order, but does not establish this app's behavior for all source conventions [S1].

Store geometry in continuous coordinates of a named source-data frame, with axis names/order and an explicit sample-index convention. Integer indices address samples. The importer records the physical anchor convention before emitting physical coordinates. Store transform ID and parameters. For verified diagonal calibration use `physical[j] = scale[j] * index[j] + translation[j]` in declared units. Screen coordinates map to data coordinates through a separate inverse display transform; never persist screen pixels. Unknown origin, spacing, orientation or units remain unknown. [W1] checks one scale/translation arithmetic example only, not any file-format convention.

Each annotation stores stable UUID, source asset UUID/hash, schema version, coordinate-frame/transform ID, kind, geometry, scope (`slice_time`, `volume`, `acquisition`), named dimension selectors, optional channel relation, style ID/revision, author and time. Slice/time objects store selectors; volume objects describe their spatial frame; an acquisition-level record may have no spatial geometry. Store source-data coordinates as canonical; derive physical coordinates only when calibration is known.

A display adjustment only updates view state. Crop, projection, resampling, axis reorder/flip, rotation or cast is an explicit operation creating a new immutable derived asset. Log parent ID/hash, operation, parameters, software version, output hash, time, interpolation/aggregation, boundary policy, dtype and clipping as relevant. NGFF 0.4 scale/translation is not a general promise for arbitrary transforms; if an operation cannot be represented faithfully, retain its app transform and refuse an export that cannot encode it explicitly [S1, §3.3–3.4].

## Save/recovery and shared storage

**Product choice.** Use a single active writer per project. Store immutable objects and versioned manifests. Write changed content to staging; flush/checksum; write a new manifest and commit pointer only after all referenced objects verify. Retain the prior valid generation. At reopen, validate the latest commit and fall back to the last complete generation; keep interrupted staging data for diagnosis/cleanup. Export to a temporary sibling path, validate, then publish without replacing a prior valid file.

Filesystem guarantees are environment-specific. Test same-filesystem rename, locking and durability on the actual local, SMB and synchronization-client storage used by the group. If atomic commit behavior is insufficient, work locally and publish immutable versioned project folders with conflict names. No concurrent writers or live merge in the initial release. Test crashes during import/copy/save/export and moving a project to a different workstation/path.

## Memory target, architecture and support boundary

The 3 GB source on an 8-core/16 GB machine is an engineering target, not demonstrated capacity. TIFF ROI access depends on tile/strip layout and codec; one compressed strip may demand a large decode. NGFF chunk geometry and Dask/cache behavior also matter. Component documentation is not proof of full-app memory or cancellation behavior [S1, S3, S4].

**Proposed limits to measure:** 512 MiB decoded-array cache, at most two decode workers, and 64 MiB maximum decoded block for direct interaction. These are product choices, not source facts. Use bounded LRU, cancel tokens and visible progress. Render only current 2D ROI/slice/time/channels; do not materialize a full volume for display changes. Use an existing pyramid only when its mapping validates. CPU is required; GPU is optional and cannot alter stored samples. If layout exceeds limits, report unsupported direct access or offer separate recorded conversion; do not silently load the whole source.

First supported formats: local single-file OME-TIFF/BigTIFF and local directory OME-NGFF 0.4 in Zarr v2; validated integer/floating samples; 2–5 named `t/c/z/y/x` axes; only calibration/orientation accepted by a tested adapter. Maintain a codec, layout, plane-order and metadata qualification matrix. Plain 2D TIFF is allowed only with explicit `YX`; calibration remains unknown unless supplied. Accept NGFF only when multiscale metadata and transforms pass validation [S1–S4].

Initially reject/defer multi-file OME-TIFF, HCS plate/well, proprietary slide/scan layouts, remote/cloud URLs, RGB/sample or unknown axes, malformed or inconsistent metadata, missing codecs, unsupported compression, and transforms that cannot be preserved. This is deliberately narrower than component format lists; no universal compatibility claim. Preserve readable metadata and precise failure reason. Let the user correct axes/calibration explicitly, retaining original metadata. Unsupported input remains a recoverable project record and does not prevent other images opening. Errors must be keyboard reachable, legible and not color-only. A partial import cannot become `ready` until required identity/metadata checks succeed.

## Export contract and honest preservation statement

**CSV table:** one row/object, with JSON-encoded geometry in a documented schema. Include project/schema IDs; source/image UUID and SHA-256; annotation UUID, kind, scope; native axes/order; source coordinates and index convention; slice/time/channel selectors; known physical coordinates/units or explicit null/reason; transform ID; style ID/revision; author/time; lineage. Paths alone are not identifiers.

**Mask overlay:** separate OME-NGFF label group when axes/transforms are representable, using integer labels and supported names/colors/properties/source relation [S1, §§3.6–3.7]. This preserves raster masks on the declared grid. Rasterized points/polygons lose vector semantics and depend on explicit grid, thickness/fill and rasterization policy; keep the table authoritative and record the policy. Do not promise all clients preserve transitional channel display or private styles.

**Small derived image:** first export is an explicit crop on the source grid, without interpolation or dtype change. Give it a new asset ID; record crop bounds and source hash. For a scale/translation frame, update translation from crop start in the named axes and compare each output sample to its source. Projection, resampling, rotation or 8-bit display rendering is a distinct derived operation recording method, interpolation/aggregation, output type and mapping. A rendered window/level image is display-derived, never original intensity data.

Report preserved sample values/dtype/axes/calibration when verified, annotation coordinates and provenance, and omitted/transformed details: TIFF byte layout/compression/IFD structure, unsupported vendor metadata, viewer-only state, transforms not encoded, or vector semantics rasterized. Hash outputs; retain originals. Standards do not ensure identical interpretation by every consumer.

## Opportunity and alternative

**Opportunity:** NGFF multiscales and integer `image-label` groups give a standards-based option for exchanging masks; a TIFF region reader can avoid mandatory full conversion for eligible TIFF. This does not preserve point/polygon semantics in a label raster or guarantee consumer behavior [S1, S3].

**Alternative:** evaluate bioformats2raw v0.12.1 for an individually chosen Bio-Formats-readable vendor file or a TIFF layout that fails direct bounded access. Convert to a separate staged NGFF-derived asset; retain/hash original and log converter version, parameters, warnings and output identity. Its release is evidence of a maintained converter, not coverage/fidelity for any lab file or proof its output matches the pinned NGFF profile [S1, S7]. Adds disk, time and Java/runtime dependencies; validate axes, calibration and pixels before adoption.

Remote storage, HCS, live multi-user merge, auto-segmentation, registration, GPU-specific acceleration and shared vocabularies remain optional. Preserve annotation UUIDs, styles and operation provenance to keep later options open; they are not MVP obligations.

## Proposed validation (all UNEXECUTED except W1)

- **Coordinates:** asymmetric tiny `z,y,x` volume, anisotropic scales, nonzero origin, axis permutation/flip; import, annotate, save/reopen and export table/mask. Require exact index round-trip and declared physical tolerance; test anchor conventions; reject unrepresentable transforms.
- **Display:** hash original and sample raw integer/float values, adjust window/level, gamma, channel and LUT, then require unchanged hash/samples. Separately verify each geometric/sample transform creates a derived asset. W1 is arithmetic only.
- **3 GB:** tiled/stripped OME-TIFF/BigTIFF and chunked NGFF across codecs, dtypes and axes; measure RSS, reads, cache, UI response and cancellation while panning/scrubbing. Proposed thresholds: <=512 MiB decoded cache, <=2 workers, <=64 MiB block and an initial <=4 GiB process RSS. Pathological strips should be rejected/rerouted. Tune with real workflow; no threshold is proven.
- **Import/error:** malformed XML/NGFF, missing linked files, codec failures, shape/axis mismatch and unknown axes; confirm untouched source, raw metadata retention, recoverable status, explicit overrides and unaffected project images.
- **Crash/recovery/relocation:** terminate at every staging/checksum/manifest/commit step and during copy/export; reopen only prior complete or new complete state. Repeat on each real filesystem. Same hash permits explicit relocation; different hash never inherits annotations silently.
- **Annotations:** keyboard-only workflow, point/polygon/mask and all scopes; undo/redo, close/reopen, edge writes, cache eviction, cancellation and backend cases. Include an app-specific regression for the failure class in napari #9047; the precedent is not an app test [S6].
- **Exports:** compare crop every sample/dtype/axis/calibration/origin; round-trip coordinates with an independent parser; check label IDs/links; source hash unchanged; show vector rasterization and omitted metadata. Fail closed on unrepresentable transforms and interrupted output.
- **Accessibility/environment:** keyboard operability/focus/accessible names; legible metadata/errors at zoom, text plus non-color status, announcements where supported; cancel responsiveness under load. Build a reproducible Python 3.12 lock and test selected Qt binding and packaging on supported OSes.

## Remaining dependencies

No dependency lock, integration build, 3 GB benchmark, crash test or second-reader export round-trip was done. Reader/codec boundaries, filesystem commit semantics, source hash/copy cost, physical sample anchors/orientations, NGFF writer-version compatibility and annotation interoperability remain gates. `leads.json` separates these unresolved critical items from optional ideas. W1 is not evidence that any application component behaves correctly.
